So I came across this oddity creating a routine to clear a form (not reset it, but clear it). I'm not sure why the .defaultProperty is "hard linked" to the value attribute, but it most definitely appears to be.
Run this example (in any browser other than IE):


var myText1 = document.getElementById('Text1');
alert('original\ndef = ' + myText1.defaultValue + '\n val = ' + myText1.value);
myText1.value = null;
alert('.value=null\ndef = ' + myText1.defaultValue + '\n val = ' + myText1.value);

var myText2 = document.getElementById('Text2');
alert('original\ndef = ' + myText2.defaultValue + '\n val = ' + myText2.value);
myText2.removeAttribute('value');
alert('.removeAttribute(\'value\')\ndef = ' + myText2.defaultValue + '\n val = ' + myText2.value);

var myRange1 = document.getElementById('Range1');
myRange1.defaultValue = myRange1.value;
alert('original\ndef = ' + myRange1.defaultValue + '\n val = ' + myRange1.value);
myRange1.removeAttribute('value');
alert('.removeAttribute(\'value\')\ndef = ' + myRange1.defaultValue + '\n val = ' + myRange1.value);
<form id="frmForm">
  <table>
    <tr>
      <td>Text1</td>
      <td>
        <input id="Text1" type="text" value="1" />
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Text2</td>
      <td>
        <input id="Text2" type="text" value="2" />
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Range1</td>
      <td>
        <input id="Range1" type="range" min="0" max="10" style="width:155px;" />
      </td>
    </tr>
  </table>
</form>



Notice that the Test1 get's a defaultValue based on it's value attribute, and just setting the value property to null leaves the defaultValue property alone.
But for Text2, .removeAttribute('value') is used, and both the value and default value are changed.
Now in the case of certain form elements, like type="range", the value property is auto-set if you do not specify a value attribute. In this case it is auto-set to the mid-way point between min and max, which in this case is 5. Please note that this does not set the defaultValue property in any way, so I manually set it. But when .removeAttribute('value') is used now only the defaultValue property appears to be changed... most likely due to the fact the range's value is being auto-set back to 5.
These types of inconsistencies require special handling and are a bit of a headache if you are unaware if them, as I was...
In my humble opinion, the value attribute, value property, and defaultValue property should be treated as separate entities after initialization and changing one should not change the other unless specifically implemented by the developer.
Can anyone give me a reason for why these were implemented in this way? Seems like this is a bit of an oversight, and really should be corrected.

